### Evidence-Based Interventions for Priority 5

#### Intervention 5.1: Structured Medication Review for High-Risk Patients by Clinical Pharmacists in Primary Care

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Organisational

**Mechanism:** Clinical pharmacists embedded in PCNs conduct structured medication reviews for patients with multimorbidity and polypharmacy; identify medications causing adverse drug reactions (ADRs) and drug-drug interactions that precipitate A&E attendance (e.g., NSAIDs in renal impairment, anticoagulant over-coagulation, insulin-related hypoglycaemia); optimise regimens for chronic disease control; reduce medication-related harm which accounts for an estimated 6.5% of hospital admissions

**Resources Required:**
- *Medium resources:* Clinical pharmacist posts in PCNs (NHS Long Term Plan funded); structured review templates; access to GP records and secondary care medication history; STOPP/START criteria tool

**Implementation Requirements:**
- PCN clinical pharmacist employment (NHS Long Term Plan pathway)
- Prioritisation of patients with polypharmacy (≥10 medications) and recent A&E attendance
- Structured medication review tool (STOPP/START criteria validated)
- Communication pathway with GP for medication changes
- Patient consent and education about medication changes

**Strength of Evidence:**
- **Peer-reviewed:** Howard et al. (2007, BMJ) — estimated 6.5% of UK hospital admissions are medication-related (real and widely cited study, I am confident in this reference); Payne et al. (2014) demonstrated medication burden in multimorbidity; STOPP/START criteria validated by O'Mahony et al. (2015, Age and Ageing — I am moderately confident in this reference); NHS IMPACT pharmacist review evidence growing through PCN evaluation studies
- **Government/NHS:** NHSE Clinical Pharmacist in General Practice programme; NHS Long Term Plan pharmacy commitments; NICE NG5 (Medicines optimisation)
- **Other:** Royal Pharmaceutical Society (RPS) medicines optimisation guidance; International Pharmaceutical Federation frameworks

**Feasibility:** Moderate-high — clinical pharmacist programme already underway in PCNs; scaling and focus on high-risk patients achievable within 2-3 years
**Barriers:** Pharmacist workforce; GP engagement with pharmacist recommendations; patient adherence to medication changes; record access
**Metrics:** Rate of medication-related A&E attendances; inappropriate medication prescribing rate (STOPP criteria); ADR-related admission rate; polypharmacy prevalence and review rate

**Evidence Classification: Strong evidence for medication review concept; Moderate evidence for A&E attendance reduction specifically**

---

#### Intervention 5.2: COPD Proactive Case Management and Personalised Action Plans

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Organisational

**Mechanism:** Patients with COPD GOLD stage 3-4 or frequent exacerbators receive proactive structured review every 3-6 months by specialist respiratory nurse or GP with special interest in respiratory medicine; personalised written action plan enables patient self-management and early treatment initiation; standing prescription for antibiotics/prednisolone reduces time-to-treatment; vaccination optimisation; smoking cessation support; this reduces exacerbation severity and prevents A&E attendance

**Resources Required:**
- *Medium resources:* Respiratory nurse specialist capacity; structured review templates; spirometry access; personalised action plan development time; standing prescription protocols

**Implementation Requirements:**
- Identification of all COPD GOLD 3-4 patients in practice registers
- Structured proactive review protocol
- Personalised action plan template (BTS/NICE format)
- Standing prescription protocol for antibiotics/steroids (requires GP sign-off)
- Self-management education programme (e.g., SPACE for COPD)
- Linkage to pulmonary rehabilitation

**Strength of Evidence:**
- **Peer-reviewed:** Bucknall et al. (2012, BMJ) — self-management intervention in COPD showing reduction in hospital admissions (I am moderately confident in this specific paper); Trappenburg et al. (2011) and Effing et al. (Cochrane) on COPD self-management reducing admissions; Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) guidelines evidence base
- **Government/NHS:** NICE NG115 (COPD in adults, 2019 updated); NHS England COPD service specification; NHSE Respiratory programme
- **Other:** British Thoracic Society COPD Guidelines; Global Initiative for COPD (GOLD) 2024 report

**Feasibility:** Moderate — primary care capacity is constrained; achievable within 2-4 years with PCN development
**Barriers:** Practice nurse capacity; patient engagement; pulmonary rehabilitation waiting times; smoking cessation service access
**Metrics:** COPD emergency admission rate; exacerbation rate; action plan possession rate; pulmonary rehabilitation completion rate; smoking cessation rate

**Evidence Classification: Moderate-to-strong evidence**

---

#### Intervention 5.3: Heart Failure Community Nurse Specialist Programme and Post-Discharge Follow-Up

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Organisational

**Mechanism:** Heart failure specialist nurses (HFSNs) manage patients with HF in the community; optimise guideline-directed medical therapy (GDMT); rapidly respond to weight gain/decompensation symptoms; prevent emergency admissions through early intervention; post-discharge follow-up within 2 weeks reduces early readmission

**Resources Required:**
- *Medium resources:* HFSN posts (typically 1 WTE per 100-150 HF patients); caseload management system; echocardiography and BNP access; drug titration protocols under consultant oversight

**Implementation Requirements:**
- HFSN post business case and commissioning
- Defined caseload with electronic patient management
- Post-discharge 2-week follow-up protocol
- Patient access to HFSN advice line
- GDMT optimisation protocol (ACE inhibitor/ARB/ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i)

**Strength of Evidence:**
- **Peer-reviewed:** Blue et al. (2001, BMJ) — specialist nurse intervention in heart failure showing reduced readmission and mortality (I am reasonably confident this is a real BMJ paper); Stewart et al. multiple papers on nurse-led HF management; Cochrane reviews on multidisciplinary management of HF (Feltner et al. and others); NICE NG106 evidence base includes HFSN evidence
- **Government/NHS:** NICE NG106 (Chronic heart failure in adults, 2018 updated 2023); NHS England Heart Failure audit; NHSE Cardiovascular Disease Prevention programme
- **Other:** British Society for Heart Failure guidance; ESC Heart Failure Guidelines

**Feasibility:** Moderate — HFSN workforce is limited; training pipeline needed; achievable within 3-5 years for full expansion
**Barriers:** HFSN workforce shortage; GP engagement; drug titration scope of practice issues; echocardiography capacity
