# PRIORITY 10: Urgent Treatment Centre Development and Minor Illness Diversion
**Composite Score: 76/100 | Confidence Rating: MODERATE**

### Why This Qualifies

- **Magnitude:** 30-40% of A&E attendances may be manageable in lower-acuity settings (though this figure is debated); even a partial diversion has significant impact on A&E waiting times
- **Evidence:** Mixed for UTCs specifically; growing for Pharmacy First; NHS 111 clinical triage has evidence base
- **NHS Alignment:** UTCs mandated in NHS Long Term Plan; Pharmacy First a 2024 priority
- **Timeline:** Many UTCs already operational; focus on quality and appropriate streaming
- **Equity:** Risk of inequitable access; must be mitigated

---

### Evidence-Based Interventions for Priority 10

#### Intervention 10.1: Pharmacy First Programme Scale-Up for Minor Illness

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Policy

**Mechanism:** Community pharmacists assess and treat seven common conditions (sinusitis, sore throat, earache, infected insect bite, impetigo, shingles, UTI in women) without GP referral; antibiotic supply under patient group direction; this diverts patients with minor illness away from both GP and A&E, improving access and reducing demand. Launched in NHS England in January 2024

**Resources Required:**
- *Low-medium resources:* Pharmacy infrastructure largely already exists; clinical training and accreditation for pharmacists; IT system for consultation recording; patient group direction for antibiotic supply; NHS payment framework for consultations

**Implementation Requirements:**
- Pharmacist training and accreditation for Pharmacy First conditions
- NHS-commissioned consultation fee structure
- IT recording and GP communication system
- Public awareness campaign to direct patients to pharmacy
- Quality monitoring and safety surveillance

**Strength of Evidence:**
- **Peer-reviewed:** Mercer et al. and evidence for community pharmacist minor illness consultations generally positive; Murray et al. on NHS Minor Ailment Scheme evidence; UTI management by pharmacist evidence: O'Brien et al. (2019) demonstrated safety and acceptability — *I flag moderate confidence in this specific reference*; Pharmacy First is too new (2024) to have full impact evidence
- **Government/NHS:** NHS England Pharmacy First service specification (2024); NHSE Pharmacy Integration Fund evaluation reports; NHS Digital Pharmacy First consultation data (early 2024 data)
- **Other:** Pharmaceutical Journal reports; Royal Pharmaceutical Society guidance; PSNC (Pharmaceutical Services Negotiating Committee) evidence

**Feasibility:** High — infrastructure exists; rapidly scalable; achievable within 1-3 years for full national scale-up
**Barriers:** Public awareness; pharmacist consultation time (competing with dispensing); referral back to GP when beyond scope; antibiotic supply governance
**Metrics:** Pharmacy First consultation volume; GP consultation rate for the seven conditions; A&E attendance rate for minor illness conditions; antibiotic prescribing rates; adverse outcome rate

**Evidence Classification: Moderate evidence for community pharmacy minor illness; Early-stage evidence for Pharmacy First specifically**

---

#### Intervention 10.2: NHS 111 Clinical Triage Enhancement with Clinician Callback

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Organisational/Policy

**Mechanism:** NHS 111 with integrated clinician callback (GP, nurse, paramedic, or pharmacist callback within 30-60 minutes for appropriate cases) enables telephone assessment and management of conditions that would otherwise result in A&E self-referral; provides safe home management advice, community prescription direction, or UTC booking; reduces inappropriate 999 calls and walk-in A&E attendance for minor conditions

**Resources Required:**
- *Medium resources:* Clinician capacity for callback (GP federation, urgent care provider); integrated IT systems between 111 and callback provider; UTC booking system integration; pharmacy referral pathway

**Implementation Requirements:**
- NHS 111 clinical hub staffing with GP/clinical pharmacist capacity
- Integrated UTC booking system (patients booked directly from 111)
- Real-time capacity data feed from UTCs to 111 system
- Clinical governance for telephone advice and prescribing
- Public confidence campaign for 111 as first point of contact

**Strength of Evidence:**
- **Peer-reviewed:** Katterl et al. and multiple studies on telephone triage; Eastwood et al. on NHS Direct/111; Blank et al. (2012) on telephone triage safety; NHS 111 evaluation studies by Turner et al. — *I flag moderate confidence in specific references*; GP callback from 111 demonstrated to reduce hospital attendances in multiple evaluations
- **Government/NHS:** NHSE NHS 111 First evaluation (NHS England, 2021); NHS 111 clinical model guidance; NHSE UEC Recovery Plan specific NHS 111 commitments
- **Other:** RCEM 111 First campaign evaluations; The Health Foundation analysis of 111

**Feasibility:** High for existing 111 infrastructure; medium for clinical callback capacity; achievable within 1-3 years for scale-up
**Barriers:** Clinician callback capacity; public trust in 111 advice; integration with out-of-hours GP; patient choice to attend A&E regardless
**Metrics:** 111 call volume and disposition (advice, callback, UTC booking, 999 referral, A&E referral); 111-attributed A&E attendance rate; patient satisfaction; safety events (adverse outcomes following 111 advice)

**Evidence Classification: Moderate evidence; National programme evaluation data available**

